  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nLong range models continue to suppress the upper level ridge\ntowards the southeast CONUS in the extended period. Favorable \nsteering flow on the western flank of the high will continue to \npromote rain chances through the week. High-end moisture profiles,\n(where 0-3 km equivalent potential temperatures are above 325 \nKelvin), should let storms through the week become efficient rain \nproducers. The potential for excessive rainfall will need to be \nmonitored, and national guidance already favors the western \nPanhandles due to the higher confidence of multiple rounds of \nstorms affecting them through the week.\n\nTemperatures this week are on the decline, with highs potentially\nnot escaping the 70s on some days. This is due to our \naforementioned high-end moisture profiles creating day time cloud \ncover alongside rain-cooled air from thunderstorms. Surface winds\nmay be more southeasterly through the week as well. This aids in \nadvecting more surface moisture into the CWA opposed to warm, dry \nair advection from the southwest.\n\nRangel",
